A||||9||pontiacs4me||got the 87 running and driving now comes the problems!!!||||1||
Z||000000||pontiacs4me||02-24-2001||03:04 AM||||Ok, After 45 hours of labor I got the swap complete.  the 2.8 runs pretty good an exhaust leak at the base of the egr valve, but other than that pretty good all around.  Heres the problem.  At first I drove it around the neighborhood by my shop, and then I hit the highway to go home.  At about 50 mph the rear end of the car starts swaying around.  It seems almost uncontrolable.  Almost like it has rear steering and someone is swerving it back and forth real lightly.  I can keep the car going straight but it is kind of scary.  Anything below 50 it does fine, but when I swerve the car back and forth it acts like its on ball bearings.  It feels like it has rear steering or something.  All the carrage bolts are in real tight so they arent slopping around under there or anything.  The rear tires are pretty worn and one is a bit low, could that be the problem.  The front tires are new and in good condition, I just havent had the time to get some new ones for the back yet.  Any ideas of what this could be?  check my next post for more problems.  thanks in advance for any help.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||02-24-2001||07:08 AM||||You did get it aligned, and yes a low rear tire will greatly affect handling.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||02-24-2001||07:56 AM||||Did you have the struts loose at the lower end? If the answer is yes, they arre not tight. DO NOT DRIVE! until you can get it on an alignment rack! Extremly dangerous!||||reg||1
Z||000003||pontiacs4me||02-24-2001||10:27 AM||||Struts loose where?  I took them off with the 3 bolts on the shock tower and fed them down through the wheel well.   I didnt want to take the big bolts out below.  Is that what  you mean?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||02-24-2001||10:35 AM||||Yes, I was speaking of the big bolts at the bottom.<BR>Just check all the suspension components. ie: ball joints and inner and outer tie rod ends and control arm bushings.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||02-24-2001||11:20 AM||||A bad alignment will cause this effect.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||02-24-2001||01:11 PM||||Get new tires and a good 4-wheel alignment. Low tire pressure will cause it too. The alignment could have shifted a bit when you re-assembled everything.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mark||02-24-2001||02:43 PM||||I agree....get new rear tires and a 4-wheel alignment.  Small changes in camber result in big changes in rear toe.  Handling can be very unpredicable and dangerous if the rear wheels aren't pointing straight!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cplensdorf||02-24-2001||08:57 PM||||Check for play in the rear toe-links by grabbing tire at 3-9 o'clock.  Bad inner link will definitely cause this as rear alignment will VARY with the road.  I know the feeling.  It feels like riding in the bucket of an empty gravel hauler!!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||pontiacs4me||02-26-2001||01:24 AM||||I found the problem. I went to check the tire pressure, and found that the rear tires were at 11 and 14 lbs and the fronts were at 18 and 22.  I put them all at 35 and the car handles great now.  I drove the car all day saturday, turned out about 21 mpg in the city, alot better than my firebird (13 mpg).  Then today it started puking antifreeze.  Oh well, gives me something to do I guess.||||reg||1
